# The inertia degree of the prime of `k[x]^int` at a zero of `x`

Let `C` be a smooth proper geometrically irreducible curve over a field `k`, let `x ∈ K(C)` be
transcendental over `k`, and let `Q` be a place at which `x` vanishes.  Write `A = k[x] ⊆ K(C)`
for the subalgebra generated by `x`, `B = A^int` for its integral closure in `K(C)`, and
`q = B ∩ 𝔪_Q` for the prime of `B` attached to `Q` (`Work.CurveBasics.Order.kxPrimeOfPlace`).

The main result of this file is that the inertia degree of `q` over `A` is the degree `[κ(Q) : k]`
of the place:

  `inertiaDeg_kxPrimeOfPlace_eq_finrank : f(q | (x)) = [κ(Q) : k]`.

The proof combines three computations.

* `isDedekindDomain_kxClosure`: `B` is a Dedekind domain.  Noetherianity comes from Krull–Akizuki
  (`Work.CurveBasics.Order.isNoetherianRing_integralClosure_of_isFractionRing`), Krull dimension
  ≤ 1 from `Ring.DimensionLEOne.integralClosure`, integral closedness from
  `integralClosure.isIntegrallyClosedOfFiniteExtension` and `K(C)` is the fraction field of `B`.
  Note that this does *not* use the finiteness of `B` over `k[x]`.

* `inertiaDeg_kxPrimeOfPlace_eq_finrank_quotient`: since `q` lies over the maximal ideal
  `(x) = A ∩ 𝔪_Q` of `A`, it is itself maximal, so `Ideal.inertiaDeg_eq_of_isMaximal` computes the
  inertia degree as the dimension of `B ⧸ q` over `A ⧸ (x)`.  The isomorphism `A ⧸ (x) ≃+* k` given
  by evaluation of a polynomial at its constant term (`Curve.kxEvalZero`, whose kernel is `(x)` by
  `Curve.kx_span_gen_eq_ker`) turns this into `finrank k (B ⧸ q)`.  The compatibility of the two
  `k`-algebra structures is the identity `f ≡ f(0) mod q` for `f ∈ A`, which holds because
  `f - f(0) ∈ (x) ⊆ q`.

* `kernelPrimeResidueField_finrank`: the natural map `B → κ(Q) = 𝓞_Q/𝔪_Q` is surjective
  (`kxClosure_residue_surjective_aux`) with kernel `q`, so it induces an isomorphism
  `B ⧸ q ≃+* κ(Q)` of `k`-algebras, whence `finrank k (B ⧸ q) = finrank k κ(Q) =
  C.hom.residueDegree Q.point` (`Work.CurveBasics.Place.degree_eq_finrank`), the last equality
  being `Q.degree = [κ(Q) : k]`.
-/
